Note that $w$ and $(w')^{-1}$ are both left-weakly decomposable with respect to $i=4$ and hence $w$, $w'$ are both weakly decomposable.  We highlight in blue how their permutation matrices are ``almost" block diagonal.  The corresponding weakly decomposing factorizations are
$$w=(s_3s_4s_2s_3s_1)\cdot(s_6s_5)\quad\text{and}\quad w'=(s_6s_5)\cdot(s_4s_2s_3s_4s_1s_2).$$

