\label{thm:overlaps}
Let $q\geq 1$.  The following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The staircase diagram $\mathcal{D}$ avoids $q$-overlaps.
    \item The permutation $\Lambda(\mathcal{D})$ avoids the patterns $\Lambda(\mathcal{O}v^{\pm}_q)$.
\end{enumerate}

If $\mathcal{D}$ contains a $q$-overlap, then \Cref{lem:overlaps_1} implies $\Lambda(\mathcal{D})$ contains one of $\Lambda(\mathcal{O}v^{+}_q)$ or $\Lambda(\mathcal{O}v^{-}_q)$.  

For the converse, we assume that $\mathcal{D}=\{B_1,\ldots, B_k\}$ avoids $q$-overlaps and proceed by induction on $k$.  First, if $k=1$, then $\mathcal{D}$ vacuously avoids $q$-overlaps and $\Lambda(\mathcal{D})=u_{B_1}$ clearly avoids $\Lambda(\mathcal{O}v^{\pm}_q)$.  Now suppose $k>1$ and let $\mathcal{D}':=\{B_1,\ldots,B_{k-1}\}$ denote the sub-diagram of $\mathcal{D}$ removing $B_k$.  Note that $\mathcal{D}'$ also avoids $q$-overlaps, so by induction, the permutation $\Lambda(\mathcal{D}')$ avoids $\Lambda(\mathcal{O}v^{\pm}_q)$.  Suppose $B_k$ is maximal in $\mathcal{D}$ and let $q':=|B_{k-1}\cap B_k|$ denote the overlap between $B_{k-1}$ and $B_k$.  In \Cref{fig:Overlaps_1}, we display the matrices of $\Lambda(\mathcal{D}')$ and $\Lambda(\mathcal{D})$ following \Cref{fig:matrix_of_sub_D} noting that region $A_2$ has $q'+1$ rows. Since $\Lambda(\mathcal{D}')$ avoids $\Lambda(\mathcal{O}v^{+}_q)$, any containment of $\Lambda(\mathcal{O}v^{+}_q)$ in $\Lambda(\mathcal{D})$ must involve region $A_3$.  Furthermore, region $A_3$ is decreasing and hence, any containment of $\Lambda(\mathcal{O}v^{+}_q)$ in $\Lambda(\mathcal{D})$ must also use $q+1$ entries from region $A_2$.  If $\Lambda(\mathcal{D})$ contains $\Lambda(\mathcal{O}v^{+}_q)$, then $q\leq q'$.  But $\mathcal{D}$ avoids $q$-overlaps by assumption and therefore $q'<q$.  This implies $\Lambda(\mathcal{D})$ avoids $\Lambda(\mathcal{O}v^{+}_q)$.  

