\label{lem:Dyck_height_valleys}
    Let $\mathcal{D}=\{B_1,\ldots,B_k\}$ be a fully supported, increasing chain on $[n]$.  Then the following are true:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\operatorname{DyckP}(\mathcal{D})$ has $k$ peaks, one corresponding to each block $B_i$.  Moreover, the height of the peak associated to $B_i$ is $|B_i|$.
        \item $\operatorname{DyckP}(\mathcal{D})$ has $k-1$ valleys, one corresponding to each pair $(B_i,B_{i+1})$.  Moreover, the height of the valley associated to $(B_i,B_{i+1})$ is $|B_i\cap B_{i+1}|$.
    \end{enumerate}

\label{prop:counting_fs_inc_Catalan}
    Let $\mathcal{D}=\{B_1,\ldots,B_k\}$ be a fully supported, increasing chain on $[n]$.  Then $\mathcal{D}$ is strongly connected if and only if the height of each valley in $\operatorname{DyckP}(\mathcal{D})$ is at least one.  

    As a consequence, the total number of fully supported, strongly connected, increasing chains on $[n]$ is the Catalan number $C_{n-1}$.
